      "text": "The given parametric equations are:\n\\[ x = 2 \\sin t \\]\n\\[ y = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\nFirst, we need to find the length of the curve from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\). The formula for the arc length \\( L \\) of a parametric curve \\((x(t), y(t))\\) from \\( t = a \\) to \\( t = b \\) is:\n\\[ L = \\int_a^b \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} \\, dt \\]\n\nWe start by computing the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\\[ \\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} (2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t \\]\n\\[ \\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt} (2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t \\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = (2 \\cos t)^2 + (-2 \\sin t)^2 \\]\n\\[ = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t \\]\n\\[ = 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) \\]\n\nWe know that \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\), so:\n\\[ \\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2 = 4 \\]\n\nThus, the integrand simplifies to:\n\\[ \\sqrt{\\left( \\frac{dx}{dt} \\right)^2 + \\left( \\frac{dy}{dt} \\right)^2} = \\sqrt{4} = 2 \\]\n\nThe arc length \\( L \\) from \\( t = 0 \\) to \\( t = \\pi \\) is:\n\\[ L = \\int_0^\\pi 2 \\, dt \\]\n\nWe now evaluate this integral:\n\\[ L = 2 \\int_0^\\pi 1 \\, dt \\]\n\\[ L = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_0^\\pi \\]\n\\[ L = 2 (\\pi - 0) \\]\n\\[ L = 2\\pi \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
