      "text": "To find the length of the parametric curve described by \\((x, y) = (2 \\sin t, 2 \\cos t)\\) from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\), we will use the formula for the arc length of a parametric curve:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- \\(x = 2 \\sin t\\)\n- \\(y = 2 \\cos t\\)\n\nFirst, we need to find the derivatives \\(\\frac{dx}{dt}\\) and \\(\\frac{dy}{dt}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dx}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\sin t) = 2 \\cos t\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\frac{dy}{dt} = \\frac{d}{dt}(2 \\cos t) = -2 \\sin t\n\\]\n\nNext, we substitute these derivatives into the arc length formula:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{\\left(2 \\cos t\\right)^2 + \\left(-2 \\sin t\\right)^2} \\, dt\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside the square root:\n\n\\[\n\\left(2 \\cos t\\right)^2 + \\left(-2 \\sin t\\right)^2 = 4 \\cos^2 t + 4 \\sin^2 t\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t)\n\\]\n\nUsing the Pythagorean identity \\(\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t = 1\\):\n\n\\[\n4 (\\cos^2 t + \\sin^2 t) = 4 \\cdot 1 = 4\n\\]\n\nThus, the arc length integral simplifies to:\n\n\\[\nL = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} \\sqrt{4} \\, dt = \\int_{0}^{\\pi} 2 \\, dt\n\\]\n\nNow, we evaluate the integral:\n\n\\[\nL = 2 \\int_{0}^{\\pi} dt = 2 \\left[ t \\right]_{0}^{\\pi} = 2 (\\pi - 0) = 2\\pi\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the length of the parametric curve from \\(t = 0\\) to \\(t = \\pi\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2\\pi}\n\\]",
